**Abstract.** Superseded. Objectives, Use Cases UC04 and UC05, Premises and Claims of the first concept, morning personal care. Replaced by [[b1 Pair walk and walk alone>>doc:tmp\: Pitch.2 Specification.b Use Cases with Claims and Premises.b1 Mindful Walk.WebHome]]. Kept for traceability; IDs shifted to follow the Walking with meaning set.

= Objective  [OBJ-10] =

//The bridge between Foundation and Specification. Per the verbatim Function definition, a Function is specified to contribute to an operational objective, a human-factors objective, or a Human Value, so this is where Foundation knowledge becomes something a Function can be traced to.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|OBJ-10
|Name|Activity reaches completion without care-worker prompting carrying it
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Derived from the Goal recorded on SA-08, and from PS-07's account of how that Goal currently fails.

Literature: Rogers et al. (1999) found that systematic graduated prompting of nursing-home residents with dementia produced a 26% increase in self-dressing and significantly fewer physical assists than usual care, in a severely impaired cohort (mean MMSE 6.07). That establishes the effect direction is achievable.

No primary data collected by this project. SA-08's own Evidence basis rests on literature and group reasoning only.
)))
|Statement|The resident reaches the last step of the tooth-brushing activity, and the verbal prompting a care worker currently has to supply is no longer what carries them there.
|Type|Operational
|Derived from|Goal on SA-08 ("maintain oral hygiene as part of the resident's normal morning routine, with as much independence as possible")
|Priority|Must. This is the Goal SA-08 states, and the point at which PS-07 breaks down.

== Graph edges ==

derived-from → Goal (SA-08); served-by → F7, F8, F9; referenced-by → UC04-F8-CL15, UC04-F8-CL16, UC04-F9-CL20.

= Objective  [OBJ-11] =

//The bridge between Foundation and Specification.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|OBJ-11
|Name|A genuine opportunity to act before assistance arrives
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Derived from HV-09 and HV-12, and from VT-10's current resolution.

Literature: Self-determination theory (Ryan & Deci, 2000) for the value itself. For why this matters specifically in dementia care, Kitwood (1997) names //disempowerment// - "not allowing or enabling a person to use the abilities they still have" - as one of the detractors that erode personhood.

No primary data collected by this project.
)))
|Statement|At each step of the activity, the resident has had a real opportunity to act on their own before any assistance arrives.
|Type|Value-derived
|Derived from|HV-09 Autonomy and HV-12 Competence. Informed by VT-10's resolution, which already states the trade-off this Objective sits on.
|Priority|Must. Without it, OBJ-10 could be satisfied by a System that simply narrates every step - which is what the existing workaround recorded on SA-08 already does, and what PS-07 identifies as the problem rather than the solution.

== Graph edges ==

derived-from → HV-09, HV-12; served-by → F8; referenced-by → UC04-F8-CL15, UC04-F8-CL17, UC04-F8-CL18; narrated-by → OS-04.

= Objective  [OBJ-12] =

//The bridge between Foundation and Specification.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|OBJ-12
|Name|Difficulty resolved where it appears, before it becomes distress
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Derived from HFC-11.

Literature: de Werd et al. (2013) review error-prevention techniques for teaching everyday tasks to people with dementia; Rogers et al. (1999) found disruptive behaviours //decreased// significantly under systematic prompting. Both support the principle that assistance timing matters to distress.

Contested on direction - the same literature favours preventing the error rather than allowing it, which is not what F8 does. See Open item O-16.

No primary data collected by this project.
)))
|Statement|Where the resident cannot continue unaided, the difficulty is resolved at the step where it appears, rather than accumulating into confusion or distress.
|Type|Human-factors
|Derived from|HFC-11 Adaptive assistance, competence and distress
|Priority|Must. This is the other side of OBJ-11; VT-10 is the trade-off between the two, and a design that serves only one of them has not resolved that tension.

== Graph edges ==

derived-from → HFC-11; served-by → F8, F9; referenced-by → UC04-F8-CL16, UC04-F8-CL18, UC04-F9-CL20.

= Objective  [OBJ-13] =

//The bridge between Foundation and Specification.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|OBJ-13
|Name|Support without the feeling of being watched
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Derived from HV-11, and from the scope decision recorded in PS-08's out-of-scope field ("we only report a failure in completing the task, we avoid surveilling the patient in general").

Literature: Niemeijer et al. (2010), a systematic review of 79 papers on the ethical and practical acceptability of surveillance technology in residential care for people with dementia. Niemeijer et al. (2015) then asked residents themselves, and concluded such technology can contribute to autonomy //only// when embedded in a person-centred approach - which is the condition this Objective encodes.

No primary data collected by this project.
)))
|Statement|The resident is supported through the activity without experiencing it as being watched, and without detail about a personal-care activity reaching anyone who does not need it.
|Type|Value-derived
|Derived from|HV-11 Privacy. Bounded by VT-09's resolution, and explained by HFC-10.
|Priority|Should. It constrains how F7 and F10 may be built rather than adding a capability of its own - but the constraint is hard, because tooth brushing is a personal-care activity.

== Graph edges ==

derived-from → HV-11; served-by → F7, F9, F10; referenced-by → UC04-F7-CL21, UC04-F10-CL22; narrated-by → OS-05.

= Objective  [OBJ-14] =

//The bridge between Foundation and Specification.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|OBJ-14
|Name|Prompting that does not talk down to the resident
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Derived from HV-10's recognition criteria, which names infantilisation explicitly.

Literature: Kitwood (1997) lists //infantilisation// - "treating the person like a child; using condescending language or gestures toward an adult" - among the 17 detractors of personhood that make up what he called malignant social psychology in dementia care. //Outpacing// (giving information or choices too quickly) is a second detractor directly relevant to how F8 paces a prompt.

Robot-specific support: Lee et al. (2016) on ageing stereotypes embedded in assistive robot design; Furlanis et al. (2026) for why an embodied system carries this risk differently from a screen, via their "embodied treatment" dimension - gaze, timing, tone. See Open item O-19.

No primary data collected by this project.
)))
|Statement|The resident is prompted through the activity without being addressed as less capable than they are.
|Type|Value-derived
|Derived from|HV-10 Dignity
|Priority|Should. Distinct from OBJ-11: OBJ-11 is about whether the opportunity to act exists, this is about how the prompt reads when it does arrive. A System could serve one and violate the other.

== Graph edges ==

derived-from → HV-10; served-by → F8; referenced-by → UC04-F8-CL19.

= Objective  [OBJ-15] =

//The bridge between Foundation and Specification.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|OBJ-15
|Name|The care worker's time goes where it is needed, and care continues at handover
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Derived from the Goal recorded on SA-10 (the care worker's morning round), added to close Open item O-12 and O-23.

Literature: Rogers et al. (1999) found that graduated prompting of nursing-home residents with dementia took considerably more caregiver time than usual care - the time cost this Objective asks the System to absorb.

No primary data collected by this project.
)))
|Statement|The care worker is only pulled back to a resident when prompting has run out, the resident is distressed, or there is a safety risk, and afterwards knows what support each resident needed.
|Type|Operational
|Derived from|Goal on SA-10 ("get every resident on the round through their morning care safely and in time, giving each the support they actually need, and know afterwards what each resident needed"). Serves HV-14 Attentive care; bounded by VT-14.
|Priority|Should. It is the project's stated aim of relieving care staff, but it must not be met at the expense of OBJ-10 to OBJ-12: a System that saves staff time by letting activities go unfinished fails.

== Graph edges ==

derived-from → Goal (SA-10), HV-14; served-by → F9, F10; referenced-by → UC04-F9-CL23, UC04-F10-CL22.

= Use Case  [UC04] =

//A formal description of one distinct Goal-directed interaction between Actors and the System. One Use Case per interaction, not per screen or per Technology feature.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|UC04
|Name|Support the resident through the morning tooth-brushing routine
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Formalizes SA-08 and PS-07.

Closest prior system: COACH (Mihailidis et al., 2008) prompted six older adults with moderate-to-severe dementia through handwashing using audio and audio-video prompts, and scored what M-13 scores here - one point per step completed without human assistance. It is the nearest published precedent for this Use Case and should be cited wherever this design is defended.

No primary data collected by this project. No resident or care worker has been observed or interviewed; SA-08 and PS-07 rest on literature plus the group's own reasoning.
)))
|Objective(s) served|OBJ-10, OBJ-11, OBJ-12, OBJ-13, OBJ-14, OBJ-15
|TDP(s) and IDP(s) used|[pending Steps 12-13] A graduated-assistance TDP and a low-intrusion prompting IDP are both anticipated; the recurring structure is visible already in F8's ladder, so these should be abstracted once UC05 exists to confirm the recurrence.
|Actors|(((
ST-09 Resident with dementia (Direct) - the only Actor who interacts with the System in real time.

ST-10 Care home staff member (Direct) - receives handover from F9 and reviews the F10 record; does not operate the System during the activity.

The System - Pepper (TECH-10) for prompts and interaction, sensors on the tooth-brushing items (TECH-11) for tracking, and a staff notification channel and session record (TECH-12). See §1.3.

ST-11 Family members (Indirect) - affected by outcomes, not an Actor in this Use Case, and not a recipient of anything F10 records.
)))
|Trigger|The resident's tooth-brushing window opens - SA-08's trigger, after waking, washing and/or breakfast - and either the resident is at the sink, or the window is running down with the activity not started. Both are in scope, because PS-07's first sub-problem is initiation.
|Pre-condition|(((
The resident's SA-08 step sequence is configured, together with the assistance baseline they currently need.

The item sensors (TECH-11) are fitted and reporting, and Pepper (TECH-10) is charged and positioned at the bathroom doorway, within earshot and sight of the sink, without blocking the resident's path. Pepper's cameras are disabled and visibly covered.

A care worker is reachable within the home for F9.

Consent arrangements for ST-09 are in place, on the terms VT-09's resolution sets.
)))
|Post-condition|(((
One of the following holds:

- the resident has completed SA-08 step 9, unaided or after prompting at some level;

- a care worker has taken over following F9;

- the window has closed with the activity incomplete, and that fact is recorded.

In every case F10 holds a session record, and no step-by-step trace of the activity is retained.
)))
|Action Sequence|(((
~1. The tooth-brushing window opens. The System begins tracking the activity (F7). **Deliberate addition** - SA-08 task 1 is the resident recognising that it is time, which PS-07 shows failing.

2. The resident enters the bathroom and begins (SA-08 tasks 2-3).

2-alt. The resident has not begun by the end of the window. F8 issues an initiation prompt. If there is no response, step 9.

3. The resident performs a step; the System tracks progress (F7). SA-08 tasks 4-8.

4. A stall is detected on the current step. The System waits the configured interval and does nothing. **Deliberate addition** - the wait is the design, not an implementation delay. See OS-04, and Open item O-16 for the literature that contests it.

5. The resident resumes unaided. Return to step 3.

6. The stall persists past the interval. F8 issues the lowest-level prompt available for that step.

7. The resident resumes. F8 stops prompting; return to step 3.

8. The stall persists. F8 re-prompts one level more specific, up to the demonstration level.

8-alt. Signs of distress at any point. Remaining prompt levels are skipped; go to step 9.

9. Prompting is exhausted without progress. F9 requests care-worker assistance, passing the activity, the step reached, and the reason.

10. A safety-critical condition occurs at any point - an immediate risk of serious harm, inferred from no sensor change and no response to prompts for a configured time (TECH-11 limitation). F9 immediately, pre-empting any prompting, per VT-08's current resolution. **Deliberate addition** - in scope by group decision (Open item O-11).

11. The resident completes SA-08 step 9, or a care worker takes over, or the window closes. F10 records the session. **Deliberate addition** relative to SA-08; it corresponds to the care worker's own recording and handover task (SA-10 task 6).

**Exception handling.** If the System cannot track the activity - sensor loss, power, or an unrecognised sequence - it reports a degraded state to the care worker rather than failing silently, and the activity falls back to the existing SA-08 workarounds. Verified by UC04-F7-PR7.
)))
|Function(s) realized|F7, F8, F9, F10 - specified in full below.

== Functions ==

**Function F7** (track position in the activity sequence): the System determines which step of the SA-08 sequence the resident is currently on, and whether progress to the next step has stalled, from the resident's observable actions on the activity's own tools and fixtures - requiring the resident neither to report anything nor to operate the System. It observes the activity only, not the resident in general. Serves OBJ-10, OBJ-13. Bounded by VT-09, VT-12. Enabled by TECH-11 (item sensors) - deliberately not by Pepper's cameras (TECH-13 rejected).

**Function F8** (prompt at the lowest sufficient level, after a wait): on a detected stall, the System waits a configured interval, then issues the least specific prompt that could unblock the current step. If the stall persists it re-prompts one level more specific - orienting the resident to the activity, then naming the next action, then demonstrating it - and stops as soon as the resident resumes. Prompts address the resident by name and in the second person, phrased as an offer rather than an instruction. Serves OBJ-10, OBJ-11, OBJ-12, OBJ-14. Bounded by VT-08, VT-10, VT-11, VT-12. Enabled by TECH-10 (Pepper): spoken prompts for the orienting and naming levels, and a picture or short video on its chest tablet for the demonstration level, rather than miming with its arms (VT-11 mitigation).

//Where F8's ladder comes from. It is not invented: it is the escalation SA-08 already records as existing workarounds - verbal reminder, then naming, then demonstrating the brushing action, then physical assistance - made systematic, with physical assistance deliberately left to the care worker via F9 rather than taken on by the System. Rogers et al. (1999) is the published precedent for graduated prompting of this kind in nursing-home dementia care.//

//Where it is contested. F8 is a least-to-most ladder. The dementia ADL literature largely runs the other way, favouring errorless learning and vanishing cues - prompt early to prevent the error, then fade support across sessions (de Werd et al., 2013). The largest trial of that approach, REDALI-DEM, then found structured relearning improved ADL performance but errorless learning gave no advantage over trial-and-error learning. So the evidence neither endorses F8's direction nor rules it out.//

//**Design bet (group decision, Open item O-16).** F8 keeps its least-to-most direction as a deliberate bet, not an established finding: we expect the wait-then-escalate ladder to preserve competence (OBJ-11) without causing avoidable frustration. UC04-F8-CL18 is the adverse Claim designed to settle it. If CL18 holds, F8 is re-specified - first with a per-resident wait interval, and if that is not enough, as most-to-least with fading.//

**Function F9** (hand over to a care worker): the System requests care-worker assistance when prompting has been exhausted without progress, when the resident shows signs of distress, or when a safety-critical condition occurs. It passes only the activity, the step reached, and the reason for handover. Serves OBJ-10, OBJ-12, OBJ-13, OBJ-15. Bounded by VT-08 - whose current resolution includes the safety-critical path - and by VT-09, VT-14. Enabled by TECH-12 (notification to the care worker's work phone), with TECH-10 telling the resident that someone is coming.

**Function F10** (record the session for handover): the System records, per session, whether the activity was completed, the highest prompt level required, and whether a care worker was called. It retains no step-by-step detail of the activity itself. Serves OBJ-13, OBJ-15. Bounded by VT-09. Enabled by TECH-12 (session record), from TECH-11 data.

//Note: F10 traces to OBJ-15, derived from the care worker's own Goal on SA-10 (care continuity at handover). Previously it had no Objective; see Open item O-12.//

== Premises table ==

One row per Premise. Verification only - does the Function operate as specified.

|=Premise ID|=Function|=Evaluation Method(s) / Measure(s)|=Verification Criteria|=Step(s)
|UC04-F7-PR7|F7|EM-05, M-12. No human subjects.|(((
Accuracy and robustness. The System identifies the resident's current SA-08 step correctly in at least [threshold - O-29] of staged sessions, and detects a stall within [threshold - O-29] seconds of its onset.

On sensor loss or an unrecognised sequence, a degraded state is reported to the care worker rather than the System failing silently.
)))|1, 3, 4, exception path
|UC04-F8-PR8|F8|EM-05, M-12 (timing component). No human subjects.|(((
Timing and conformity to specified interaction behaviour. The full wait interval elapses before any prompt is issued. Prompting ascends exactly one level at a time, never skipping a level except on the distress path. Prompting stops within [threshold - O-29] seconds of the resident resuming. No prompt is repeated at the same level more than once.

The false-prompt rate matters more than it looks: mistimed prompts were the failure mode observed in COACH (Mihailidis et al., 2008), and are the mechanism by which UC04-F8-CL18 would come true.
)))|4, 6, 7, 8
|UC04-F9-PR9|F9|EM-05, M-12. No human subjects.|(((
Timing, correctness, and availability. A handover request reaches a care worker within [threshold - O-29] seconds of the triggering condition. The request carries only activity, step reached, and reason. The safety-critical path pre-empts all pending prompting rather than queueing behind it.
)))|9, 10, 8-alt
|UC04-F10-PR10|F10|EM-05, M-17 (record-content component). No human subjects.|(((
Correctness and conformity. The session record contains exactly the three specified items and no step-level trace of the activity. Records are not readable by roles outside the agreed set - ST-11 in particular is not a recipient.
)))|11

== Claims table ==

One row per Claim. Validation - does the Function produce the intended effect. Premise and Claim numbers are drawn from project-wide counters, not reset per Use Case or per Function.

|=Claim ID|=Function|=Serves Objective|=Effect|=Grounding|=Evaluation Method(s) / Measure(s)|=Validation Criteria|=Step(s)
|UC04-F8-CL15|F8|OBJ-11, OBJ-10|Positive. Increases the number of SA-08 steps the resident completes with no prompt at all, compared with the care worker's current prompt-on-hesitation pattern. Concerns ST-09, SA-08, PS-07.|HFC-11; HV-09, HV-12. Precedent: Rogers et al. (1999) for the effect; Mihailidis et al. (2008) for the same outcome measure in a prompting system.|EM-06; M-13, M-15|M-13 mean above the same residents' pre-System baseline, with no increase in M-15.|3-8
|UC04-F8-CL16|F8|OBJ-12, OBJ-10|Positive. Where the resident does stall, the graduated ladder resolves it at a lower level of assistance than current practice, which moves to naming or demonstrating at once. Concerns ST-09, ST-10, SA-08, HV-12.|HFC-11. Precedent: Rogers et al. (1999) found significantly fewer physical assists under graduated prompting.|EM-06; M-14, M-13|M-14 distribution shifted toward lower levels than baseline, at equal or better completion on M-13.|6-8
|UC04-F8-CL17|F8|OBJ-11|Positive. Increases the resident's felt sense of doing the activity themselves rather than being led through it. Concerns ST-09, HV-09.|HFC-09; HV-09. Ryan & Deci (2000) for the mechanism; Peek et al. (2014) for acceptance factors in this population.|EM-06; M-16 (self-report and observer rating)|Both parts of M-16 are required. Holds if the observer's support-of-autonomy rating is higher than the pre-System baseline, and the self-report mean is no lower than baseline. The observer rating carries the Claim; the self-report alone cannot, because resident self-reports show documented ceiling effects (Custers et al., 2013; Open item O-17). If the two disagree, report both and treat the Claim as unresolved.|3-8
|UC04-F8-CL18|F8|OBJ-11, OBJ-12|**Adverse.** For residents whose current ability falls below a step's demand, the deliberate wait before prompting increases observed frustration rather than producing independent completion. Concerns ST-09, HV-13.|HFC-11; HV-13. This tests VT-10 directly. Precedent: mistimed and false-alarm prompts in COACH (Mihailidis et al., 2008) were observed to produce general frustration and confusion - the same mechanism CL15 relies on, running the wrong way.|EM-06; M-15, M-14|Holds if M-15 (Resistiveness to Care Scale) rises above baseline for the lower-ability subgroup while M-13 does not improve for them. If it holds, the wait interval must become per-resident rather than configured once.|4, 6
|UC04-F8-CL19|F8|OBJ-14|**Adverse.** Being prompted through tooth brushing by a robot is experienced as infantilising, independently of whether it improves completion. Concerns ST-09, HV-10.|HFC-12, HFC-13; HV-10. Kitwood (1997), who names infantilisation and outpacing among the detractors that erode personhood in dementia care. Robot-specific: Lee et al. (2016) on ageing stereotypes in assistive robot design, and Furlanis et al. (2026), whose "embodied treatment" dimension covers the gaze, timing and tone F8 controls - including a case of exaggerated gentleness toward older adults read as condescension.|EM-06, EM-07; M-15, M-17|Holds if residents (EM-06 post-session interview) or care staff (EM-07) describe the prompting as talking down to the resident or as child-like (M-17 code T), or if M-15 rises above baseline during prompting. Tests VT-11. Sensitive to §1.3: Pemberton et al. (2026) suggest an expressive humanoid form would raise this risk relative to an appliance-like one, so a negative result on one embodiment does not clear the other. The selected embodiment is Pepper (TECH-10), the higher-risk form; TECH-14 is the fallback if this Claim holds.|6-8
|UC04-F9-CL20|F9|OBJ-10, OBJ-12|Positive. Reduces the share of sessions that end incomplete, by bringing a care worker in when prompting has run out instead of the activity being abandoned. Concerns ST-09, ST-10, SA-08, PS-07.|HV-13; HFC-11|EM-06; M-13|Fewer incomplete sessions than baseline. (The time effect was split out into UC04-F9-CL23; Open item O-18.)|9, 10, 8-alt
|UC04-F9-CL23|F9|OBJ-15|Positive. Reduces the care worker's prompting time per resident during the morning round, because the System carries the waiting and the lower prompt levels and the care worker is called only when needed. Concerns ST-10, SA-10, HV-14.|HV-14; HFC-11. At risk: Rogers et al. (1999) found graduated prompting took considerably //more// caregiver time than usual care - absorbing that time is the point of the System, but it is an empirical question. Tests VT-14.|EM-06, EM-07; M-18|M-18 total care-worker prompting time per session below the pre-System baseline, with completion (M-13, CL20) no worse. Holds only if both are true: saving time by letting sessions go unfinished does not count.|9, 8-alt
|UC04-F7-CL21|F7|OBJ-13|Positive. The resident does not experience the step tracking as being watched, because it requires nothing of them and observes the activity rather than the person. Concerns ST-09, HV-11.|HFC-10, HFC-13; HV-11. Niemeijer et al. (2010, 2015) - residents can experience surveillance technology as supporting autonomy, but only within a person-centred approach. Pemberton et al. (2026) sharpens the risk: older adults assigned humanoid robots the "Warden" role - oversight without attachment - which is this Claim failing by design rather than by accident.|EM-06, EM-07; M-17|No resident expressions of being watched in M-17 coding (code W, EM-06 post-session interview), and care staff judge the retained record proportionate (EM-07). Sensitive to §1.3 on the same axis as CL19: Pepper's cameras are disabled and covered, but physically present.|1, 3
|UC04-F10-CL22|F10|OBJ-13, OBJ-15|Positive. The three-item session record gives the care worker enough to adjust the support they offer, without exposing how the resident performed a personal-care activity step by step. Concerns ST-10, ST-09, HV-11.|HV-11; VT-09 resolution; Niemeijer et al. (2010) on proportionality of what is recorded.|EM-07; M-17|Care staff report the record sufficient for handover and identify nothing in it they consider intrusive.|11

== Graph edges ==

realizes → DS-04 (§2.1); serves → OBJ-10…OBJ-15; uses → TDP, IDP (pending Steps 12-13); asserts → UC04-F7-PR7, UC04-F8-PR8, UC04-F9-PR9, UC04-F10-PR10, UC04-F8-CL15…UC04-F10-CL22, UC04-F9-CL23.

= Use Case  [UC05] =

//Declared and deliberately deferred, so the scope decision is explicit rather than implicit in what happened to get written first.//

**Report Format:** §2.3 Objectives, Use Cases, Functions, Premises and Claims

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|UC05
|Name|Support the resident through the medication routine
|Status|Not Started
|Evidence basis|Would formalize SA-09 and PS-08. No primary data collected by this project.
|Scope decision|(((
SA-09 and SA-08 have different triggers and different Goals, so this is a separate Use Case rather than an alternate flow of UC04 - it is not a candidate for merging.

It is deferred for this cycle rather than dropped. Reasons:

- PS-08's out-of-scope field already narrows it heavily, including a care-home blacklist for residents on critical medication, which needs settling before Functions can be written.

- PS-08 adds a fourth sub-problem, confirmation, that has no counterpart in PS-07 - the resident failing to confirm they have taken the medication, risking a double dose. That needs its own Function and its own Claim, and possibly a safety Premise, rather than reusing F7-F10.

- Specifying it properly would roughly double the Premise and Claim count, and the medication activity will need Measures of its own on the §1.2 Measures page, numbered from M-19.

F7-F10 are expected to transfer, since both activities are stepwise sequences with initiation, sequencing and completion breakdowns. Confirming that recurrence across two Use Cases is what would justify abstracting the TDP and IDP in Steps 12-13 - so UC05 should be written before those are finalized, not after.

Group decision (Open item O-22): tooth brushing is this cycle's spine. Material already written for medication is kept for UC05 rather than discarded: DS-05 (§2.1), the tray or dispenser option TECH-15 (§1.3), and the medication-timing tension VT-13 (§1.2).
)))

= Open items =

These are gaps recorded as explicit decisions rather than left silent, per Step 14.

