|O-16|(((
F8's direction is contested by the literature. F8 is least-to-most: allow the stall, prompt minimally, then escalate. The dementia ADL literature largely favours the opposite - errorless learning with vanishing cues, prompting early to prevent the error and fading support across sessions (de Werd et al., 2013). The largest trial of that approach, REDALI-DEM (Voigt-Radloff et al., 2017; n = 161, nine 1-hour sessions over eight weeks), found that structured relearning improved ADL performance and that improvements held for six months - but that errorless learning gave no additional effect over trial-and-error learning. Note that de Werd is an author on both the review and the trial that failed to confirm it.

Two things follow. First, the evidence neither endorses F8's direction nor rules it out. Second, REDALI-DEM's participants had Alzheimer's or mixed-type dementia and were living at home, so even that null result transfers to care-home residents only cautiously.

Either F8 is re-specified as most-to-least with fading, or it stays as a deliberate bet that UC04-F8-CL18 is designed to settle - which has to be stated as a bet rather than presented as established.

Resolved (group decision: keep least-to-most, as a stated bet). F8's section now says so explicitly, names CL18 as the Claim that settles it, and states the fallback if CL18 holds: first a per-resident wait interval, then most-to-least with fading.
)))|Nothing|Closed
|O-17|(((
Resolved. UC04-F8-CL17 now requires both parts of M-16: the observer's support-of-autonomy rating carries the Claim, the self-report must be no lower than baseline, and a disagreement between them leaves the Claim unresolved. M-16's items and scoring are now recorded from the source (Custers et al., 2013).

Background: self-report of autonomy in this population is unreliable, and this is measured, not just suspected. Custers et al. (2013) found residents rated their own need fulfilment higher than trained observers did, with only weak-to-moderate agreement. Remaining caveat: that sample was physically frail residents (n = 36, mean age 80), not specifically residents with dementia.
)))|Nothing|Closed
|O-18|Resolved. UC04-F9-CL20 is now only the completion effect; the time effect is the new UC04-F9-CL23 (OBJ-15, HV-14, M-18), which holds only if care-worker time falls //and// completion is no worse.|Nothing|Closed
|O-19|(((
Resolved. The earlier TODO asked whether being prompted by a //robot// specifically, rather than a screen or a voice, changes the infantilisation effect in CL19. It does.

Furlanis et al. (2026) argue that social robots are "socially legible bodies, interpreted in context through posture, timing, tone, and spatial behavior", and give three dimensions along which bias is enacted: embodied stereotypes (appearance), embodied constraints (affordances), and embodied treatment - bias arising in real-time interaction through gaze, timing and tone. F8's prompt pacing falls in the third. Their illustrative case includes a robot that exaggerated slowness and gentleness toward older adults, which was read as condescending rather than accommodating, which is CL19's mechanism. Note this paper is explicitly conceptual, so it frames the risk rather than measuring it.

The empirical source they cite for this is Lee et al. (2016), "That robot is not for me: Addressing stereotypes of aging in assistive robot design."

Form also changes the role the robot gets assigned. Pemberton et al. (2026) interviewed 13 older adults and found they sort socially assistive robots into four roles by autonomy and relationship tolerance - Carer, Pet, Warden, Tool. Humanoid robots were assigned the "Warden" role: oversight without attachment. Older adults preferred "tool" or "pet" framings over "friend" or "assistant" to protect dignity and autonomy, and (reported there from Prakash & Rogers, 2015) preferred more mechanical-looking robots that preserve emotional distance in private contexts such as bathing. Tooth brushing is such a context.

So CL19 and CL21 are both sensitive to the §1.3 choice, and in the same direction: a humanoid, expressive form raises the infantilisation risk in CL19 //and// the being-watched risk in CL21. See Open item O-20.

Transfer limits: Pemberton et al. studied cognitively intact older adults, in a VR home setup, ageing in place - not care-home residents with dementia.
)))|CL19, CL21; feeds §1.3|Closed
|O-20|(((
Arising from O-19. The evidence points somewhere specific on the open embodiment question: for a personal-care activity, a less humanlike, appliance-like form that preserves emotional distance is better supported than an expressive humanoid one. Zsiga et al. (2013, reported in Pemberton et al., 2026) also found older adults name overreach in control and cognitive support as key barriers to acceptance - which is this System's exact category.

Two actions: (a) §1.3 should record this as the rationale for whichever Technology Option is selected, and as the reason for rejecting a humanoid one; (b) §1.2 should add an HFC on embodiment and perceived role, so CL19 grounds on a concept rather than on Claim-level citations. That would also close the remaining half of O-13.

Resolved, with a recorded tension. (a) §1.3 records this evidence: the tool-like device (TECH-14) is best supported, but the group selected Pepper (TECH-10) as the project's platform. The tension is stated on TECH-10 with mitigations - tool framing, low expressivity, demonstration on the tablet, cameras disabled and covered - and TECH-14 is the named fallback if CL19 or CL21 hold. (b) Added HFC-13 (Embodiment and perceived role), which CL19 and CL21 now ground on.
)))|Nothing|Closed
